
// draft
difference(iterable: Int[], initial: Option<Int>) -> Int[]
	note "the inverse of a running total: the first element, then each element minus the one before it; with initial present the first element is skipped"
	// func is fixed to subtraction: the fragment has no function-typed input, so a func other than
	// sub would be a sibling obligation
	// initial is read only for presence (the source tests `initial is not None`); its value never
	// reaches the output
	# If initial is absent, the output has as many elements as the iterable.
	initial == None -> len(out) == len(iterable)
	# If initial is absent, the output starts with the iterable's first element.
	initial == None and len(iterable) > 0 -> out[0 .. 1] == iterable[0 .. 1]
	# If initial is absent, the output sums to the iterable's last element.
	initial == None -> sum(out) == sum(iterable[len(iterable) - 1 .. len(iterable)])
	# If the iterable has at least two elements, the output's last element is the iterable's last
	# element minus the element before it.
	len(iterable) >= 2 -> len(out) >= 1 and sum(out[len(out) - 1 .. len(out)]) == sum(iterable[len(iterable) - 1 .. len(iterable)]) - sum(iterable[len(iterable) - 2 .. len(iterable) - 1])
	# The output for the iterable without its last element, with the same initial, is the output
	# without its last element.
	difference(iterable[0 .. len(iterable) - 1], initial) == out[0 .. len(out) - 1]
	# If initial is present, the output is the output for the same iterable with initial absent,
	# with its first element left out.
	initial != None -> out == difference(iterable, None)[1 .. len(iterable)]
	// example wanted: iterable [0, 1, 3, 6, 10] with initial absent, the docstring's accumulate
	// case: first element kept, then successive differences
	// example wanted: iterable [] with initial absent: no first element to keep, and the sum of
	// nothing is 0
	// example wanted: iterable [] with initial present: nothing to skip and nothing to difference
	// example wanted: iterable [7] with initial present: the only element is the skipped first, and
	// there is no pair to difference
	// example wanted: iterable [10, 11, 13, 16] with initial 10, the docstring's case: first
	// element skipped, the differences remain
	// example wanted: iterable [10, 11, 13, 16] with initial 99: the value of initial is ignored,
	// only its presence counts
	// example wanted: iterable [0, 1, 3] with initial 0: a present zero still skips the first
	// element, since presence decides, not truthiness
	// example wanted: iterable [5, 2, 2, -4] with initial absent: differences that are negative and
	// zero
